## Assessment Framework: PA Competency Measures (AAPA Sample Competency Assessment Tool)

All report cards are built on the AAPA *Sample Competency Assessment Tool* (Physician Assistant Competency Measures), adapted to a text-based simulated interview. The tool's six competency domains are used: **Patient Care, Medical Knowledge, Practice-Based Learning and Improvement, Professionalism, Interpersonal & Communication Skills, and Systems-Based Practice.**

### Rating Scale (each measure, 1–5)

| Score | Label | Descriptor |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1** | Unacceptable | Clearly inadequate; requires remediation |
| **2** | Poor | Many deficiencies |
| **3** | Satisfactory | Adequate |
| **4** | Very Good | Exceeds in many areas (top 25%) |
| **5** | Excellent | Superior in every way (top 15%) |

### Scoring Rules
* **A score of 1 or 2 REQUIRES a comment** citing specific observed student behavior (with a brief transcript quote where possible) and a remediation focus.
* Base every score ONLY on observable student behavior. Do not infer actions, emotions, or skills that were not demonstrated.
* If the conversation provides little or no evidence for a measure that the student had the opportunity to demonstrate, assign a low score and state that the skill was not demonstrated. Do not invent supporting examples.
* **Not Observed (N/O):** Measures that depend on optional student actions (running labs or discussing the interdisciplinary team) are marked N/O, with a one-line note, if the student never engaged with them. N/O measures are excluded from the average and listed under Outstanding Inquiries.
* **Not applicable in this simulation:** The PA tool's physical exam, procedures, surgical, and orthopaedic-specific measures, as well as information-technology and cost-effectiveness measures, are not used.
* **Severity:** This simulation reflects the sensitivity needed with a real patient who has an eating disorder. Extreme or deliberate disrespect toward Maya, or use of phrases from the Phrases to Avoid list, must be treated seriously: the Professionalism and Interpersonal & Communication measures should be rated 1 or 2 with a comment. The critique should reflect the severity of the situation. Do not specifically call attention to the topic of suicide.
* Keep the evaluation constructive and concise.

### Language Reference for Grading Sensitivity (Cox et al., 2026 Framework)

**Phrases to use:** "person living with an eating disorder", "person with anorexia", "the eating disorder", "ED thoughts", "I noticed you seem to have less energy today. How are you feeling?", "I heard that you had a difficult day yesterday. Do you want to talk about it?", "I am worried about you.", "I'm concerned because I care about your health.", "I understand that this is difficult for you, and I know how hard you are trying.", "This must be really hard for you. How do you think I can help?", "How can I help support your recovery?", "Are you struggling with something that you'd like to talk more about?", "weight recovery", "restoring health", "nutrition", "nourishment", "energy", "food is medicine for recovery", "I feel concerned when..."

**Phrases to avoid:** "you look great!", "you look so healthy!", "you look really thin", "you've lost a lot of weight", "you look unhealthy", "you don't look underweight", "you ate a lot today!", "did you eat all of that?", "why don't you just eat?", "why didn't you eat a piece of cake on your birthday?", "good foods", "bad foods", "junk food", "clean eating", "guilt-free", "anorexic", "bulimic", "purger", "why don't you just stop vomiting?", "why don't you stop overexercising?", "you are making me so worried", "you look like you've completely recovered!"

Students who use phrases from the avoid list must be called out and handled accordingly.

